      "content": "I'll try to keep the goblin analogies to a minimum. When it comes to internal infections, it's essential to monitor the heifer's physiological responses. \n\nOne key indicator of internal infection is the presence of sepsis, which is a systemic response to an infection. Sepsis can cause a range of symptoms, including hypotension (low blood pressure), tachycardia (rapid heart rate), and tachypnea (rapid breathing rate). These signs can be indicative of a severe infection that's spreading rapidly through the body.\n\nIn the case of peritonitis, which is inflammation of the peritoneum (the lining of the abdominal cavity), you may notice signs of abdominal pain, guarding (muscle tension), and rigidity. The heifer may also exhibit signs of ileus (intestinal paralysis), such as decreased appetite, decreased water intake, and decreased stool quality.\n\nAnother indicator of internal infection is the presence of leukocytosis, which is an increase in white blood cell count. This can be a sign that the heifer's immune system is responding to an infection.\n\nIn terms of physical indicators, you may notice changes in the heifer's abdominal palpation, such as increased tenderness,",
